  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1218 AM CDT Mon Jun 15 2026\n\nWV imagery indicates a zonal flow aloft prevailing across the \nWestern High Plains. Near the surface, weak high pressure is \ndrifting east through western Kansas.\n\nThunderstorm chances (30-50%) return to portions of southwest\nKansas late today as the SREF indicates a series of H5 vort \nmaxima cycling southeast off a ridge axis situated near the \nPacific Coast, and through the Colorado Rockies into the Western\nHigh Plains. Moisture return on the backside of a departing \nsurface high will push dewpoints well up into the 50s(F) in \nextreme southwest Kansas, introducing an instability axis near \nthe Colorado line ahead of a developing surface low with MUCAPE \nvalues pushing upward of 1000 to potentially 1500 J/kg. Despite \na less than robust flow aloft, steepening low/mid-level lapse \nrates combined with sufficient instability will lead to likely \nthunderstorm development across extreme southeast Colorado late \nafternoon, then spreading into southwest Kansas this evening. As\nsuggested by the HREF, the best chance for any appreciable \nrainfall will generally be southwest of a Scott City to Medicine\nLodge line where there is a 20-40% probability of 6-hr QPF \nexceeding 0.1 of an inch by late evening. Diminishing rain \nchances shift eastward Tuesday under a weakening northwest flow \naloft and weak forcing in the lower levels.\n\nWarmer temperatures are likely today as southerlies return \nbehind the aforementioned departing surface high while surface \nlow pressure develops in southeast Colorado. This will pull \nwarmer air into the area with H85 temperatures a little above \n15C in central Kansas to near 20C in extreme southwest Kansas. \nWith the HREF painting a 70-90% probability of temperatures \ntopping 80F, look for widespread afternoon highs in the 80s(F). \nThe warming trend continues Tuesday with the help of prevailing \nsoutherlies, pushing afternoon highs up into the mid 80s(F) to \nnear 90F.",
